                    {
                        "form_id": "187b7f91-4795-4b93-b3e6-f6705979fa1d",
                        "entry_id": "3341cff1-30c3-38c9-9c2c-e73930ce3dcf",
                        "tags": [
                            "accusative",
                            "singular"
                        ],
                        "form": "die Deontologie"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "48f97949-601a-3616-8310-47e250b0be06",
                        "entry_id": "3341cff1-30c3-38c9-9c2c-e73930ce3dcf",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Klasse verschiedener ethischer Theorien, gemeinsam ist eine Auffassung, die davon ausgeht, dass Handlungen auch aus sich selbst heraus (intrinsisch) gut oder schlecht sein können (man also nicht nur die Folgen/Konsequenzen betrachtet/betrachten sollte)"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "16a0b52c-153d-455f-af15-1e70d1187888",
                                "sense_id": "48f97949-601a-3616-8310-47e250b0be06",
                                "text": "Kant war ein bedeutsamer Vertreter der Deontologie."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "91711d81-d724-4fe3-b568-d47eda622eea",
                                "sense_id": "48f97949-601a-3616-8310-47e250b0be06",
                                "text": "„Bestimmte allgemein verbindliche Pflichten geben vor, welche Handlungen moralisch vertretbar sind und welche nicht (z. B. „Du sollst nicht töten!“). Für Theorien der Deontologie ist kennzeichnend, dass eine Handlung einer solchen verpflichtenden Regel entsprechen muss.“"
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "c09a3735-424f-4b8b-9cef-8a47dbc159c1",
                        "source_entry_id": "3341cff1-30c3-38c9-9c2c-e73930ce3dcf",
                        "source_sense_id": "48f97949-601a-3616-8310-47e250b0be06",
                        "source_sense_description": "Philosophie: Klasse verschiedener ethischer Theorien",
                        "target_lang_word": "义务伦理学",
                        "target_lang_code": "zh",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "5d82b9cc-a950-4dff-bf58-b6d8d9ca189f",
                        "source_entry_id": "3341cff1-30c3-38c9-9c2c-e73930ce3dcf",
                        "source_sense_id": "48f97949-601a-3616-8310-47e250b0be06",
                        "source_sense_description": "Philosophie: Klasse verschiedener ethischer Theorien",
                        "target_lang_word": "deontology",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "deontology",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6aaece59-7143-331c-b3d3-4586f900781e",
                                        "entry_id": "b4191745-d116-30cf-bea0-5209178ed437",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Synonym of ethics (“the study of principles relating to right and wrong conduct”)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "27f41098-bb17-44b9-8e7e-56a9a90df133",
                                                "sense_id": "6aaece59-7143-331c-b3d3-4586f900781e",
                                                "text": "[Matthew Hale] was no reformer: nothing better than an expounder: every thing stated by him was stated as he found it, or conceived it to be: no inquiries as to what it ought to be: in the eyes of lawyers—not to speak of the dupes—that is to say, as yet, the generality of non-lawyers—the is and the ought to be (or, as in Greek it would be so much better—the το ον and the το δεον, from which last, Ethics has received the more expressive name of Deontology), were one and indivisible."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "45b21caa-6e6f-4e6b-8aef-8fde49c9b045",
                                                "sense_id": "6aaece59-7143-331c-b3d3-4586f900781e",
                                                "text": "The principle, then, on which Deontology is grounded, is the principle of Utility; in other words, that every action is right or wrong—worthy or unworthy—deserving approbation or disapprobation, in proportion to its tendency to contribute to, or to diminish the amount of public happiness."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b70da5cc-3ee8-4a0c-b323-041e935228fe",
                                                "sense_id": "6aaece59-7143-331c-b3d3-4586f900781e",
                                                "text": "And then she discoursed with voluble blandiloquence on deontologies and morals, and pondered with imposing gravity on ancient lores, and wisdoms, and beatifications, and in all her countenance seemeth as demure as a babe asleep."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5af61b21-59eb-4617-9819-b7c7d8ccf048",
                                                "sense_id": "6aaece59-7143-331c-b3d3-4586f900781e",
                                                "text": "[T]he main concern of the last two hundred and fifty years or so of ethics has been to assess the morality of actions or rules by themselves – act-and-rule utilitarianisms and deontologies – as opposed to an interpretation of actions within a morality of being."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8f80c26a-3f2e-487c-bf74-e7dfd3373d6c",
                                                "sense_id": "6aaece59-7143-331c-b3d3-4586f900781e",
                                                "text": "William Frankena has altered [Charlie Dunbar] Broad's distinction slightly to define teleology as making consequences the \"one and only … ultimate right-making characteristic\" and to define deontology as allowing \"that there are other considerations that may make an action or rule right or obligatory besides the goodness or badness of its consequences …\"[…]."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ed916114-0102-4130-a9bb-77615b5bb057",
                                                "sense_id": "6aaece59-7143-331c-b3d3-4586f900781e",
                                                "text": "Deontologies that arise from status functions are not morally neutral. This is in part because of familiar issues such as that people are likely to rely on the observance of such deontologies, the wrongfulness of freeriding, and so on."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8c0de216-a2b9-3b50-b292-ceab485fef5c",
                                        "entry_id": "b4191745-d116-30cf-bea0-5209178ed437",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Synonym of ethics (“the study of principles relating to right and wrong conduct”).",
                                            "The normative ethical theory that the morality of an action is based on whether the action follows certain obligations or rules, rather than on its inherent goodness, consequences, etc."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "specifically",
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "91b05d0e-ca83-47e2-909c-8d45f5c42adb",
                        "source_entry_id": "3341cff1-30c3-38c9-9c2c-e73930ce3dcf",
                        "source_sense_id": "48f97949-601a-3616-8310-47e250b0be06",
                        "source_sense_description": "Philosophie: Klasse verschiedener ethischer Theorien",
                        "target_lang_word": "déontologisme",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "déontologisme",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8686ddfa-192d-3dd1-be48-97ecc0a5c513",
                                        "entry_id": "523e4e0f-ecf0-39ec-bd7a-105fdb3853bd",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Morale du devoir, dans le langage des utilitaires."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a42009ee-5601-4aec-ab28-3e71b5470c5b",
                                                "sense_id": "8686ddfa-192d-3dd1-be48-97ecc0a5c513",
                                                "text": "Le conséquentialisme est l’une des deux grandes approches qui fondent aujourd’hui la philosophie morale; la seconde, c’est le déontologisme. Conséquentialisme et déontologisme sont généralement présentés de manière antinomique; il faut en somme choisir son camp. … Ou encore, le déontologisme s’intéresse aux intentions des acteurs, ce qui implique nécessairement le rapport à une loi (fondée dans l’intuition morale, la nature humaine, la raison ou la révélation divine), alors que le conséquentialisme ne se laisse pas troubler par les intentions des acteurs et se concentre sur les conséquences de leurs actions."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
